<DetailLayout title="About This Guide" subtitle="Independent, honest, and built for travelers who want real information — not tour agency marketing." description="About the Guilin Yangshuo Travel Guide: who we are, how we research, our editorial standards, and why we built an independent travel resource for Guilin and Yangshuo.">
  <h2>Why This Guide Exists</h2>
  <p>Most Guilin Yangshuo guides are written by tour agencies trying to sell you something, or by bloggers who spent two days here five years ago and wrote a listicle. We wanted something different: a guide for independent travelers who want honest, practical, up-to-date information without the sales pitch.</p>
  <p>This guide doesn't accept sponsored content, free trips, or paid placements. If we recommend a place — a hotel, a restaurant, a route — it's because we genuinely think it's good, not because someone paid us to say so. We don't run ads, we don't sell tours, and we're not affiliated with any travel agency or hotel group.</p>

  <h2>Who We Are</h2>
  <p>We're a small team of travel writers and researchers who have spent years exploring China — not just the highlights, but the side streets, the local noodle shops, the guesthouses that don't show up on Booking.com. Between us, we've collectively spent over two years living and traveling in the Guilin-Yangshuo region, and we return regularly to verify information and discover what's changed.</p>
  <p>Our contributors have backgrounds in journalism, travel writing, and Chinese language study. We're not "influencers" chasing views or affiliate commissions. We're people who genuinely love this landscape and want other travelers to experience it well.</p>

  <h2>How We Research</h2>
  <h3>On-the-Ground Verification</h3>
  <p>Every page is based on on-the-ground experience — we've spent months exploring Guilin, Yangshuo, and the surrounding countryside on bikes, on bamboo rafts, on foot, and over many bowls of rice noodles. Prices are verified regularly. Routes are walked. Restaurants are eaten at (repeatedly). We don't write about a place we haven't been to.</p>
  <h3>Price Accuracy</h3>
  <p>All prices in this guide were verified in 2026. We check prices every 6 months — ticket offices, hotel rates, restaurant menus, transport fares. If you find a price that's off, <a href="mailto:hello@guilin-yangshuo.com" class="text-accent underline">tell us</a> and we'll fix it within a week.</p>
  <h3>No Sponsored Content</h3>
  <p>We have never accepted payment for a recommendation and never will. If a restaurant, hotel, or tour operator offers us a free meal or stay in exchange for a review, we decline. Our editorial independence is the entire point of this guide.</p>

  <h2>What We Cover</h2>
  <p>This isn't just a destination guide. We cover the stuff that actually stresses people out about visiting China:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><a href="/china-visa" class="text-accent underline">Visa applications</a></strong> — who needs one, how to apply, transit options</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/alipay-wechat-setup" class="text-accent underline">Mobile payment setup</a></strong> — Alipay and WeChat Pay for foreigners</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/china-internet-guide" class="text-accent underline">Internet access</a></strong> — eSIMs, VPNs, and staying connected</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/how-to-book-china-trains" class="text-accent underline">Train booking</a></strong> — 12306, Trip.com, and ticket classes</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/survival-chinese-phrases" class="text-accent underline">Language barriers</a></strong> — essential phrases with audio</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/guilin-food" class="text-accent underline">Food</a></strong> — what to eat, where, and how to order</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Because knowing how to find Moon Hill doesn't help if you can't get online or pay for your lunch.</p>

  <h2>Editorial Standards</h2>
  <h3>Accuracy</h3>
  <p>We fact-check every page before publication. When regulations change (as they frequently do in China — visa rules, VPN availability, app functionality), we update within 48 hours. The "Last Updated" date at the bottom of each page shows when it was last revised.</p>
  <h3>Honesty</h3>
  <p>If something is overrated, we say so. If West Street is touristy and overpriced, we tell you — and then tell you which side street has the real food. If a "must-see" attraction is skippable, we explain why. We'd rather you have a great trip than hit every checkbox on a generic itinerary.</p>
  <h3>Original Research</h3>
  <p>We don't rewrite other people's blog posts. Every route, recommendation, and price in this guide comes from our own first-hand experience or from trusted local contacts who live in the region year-round.</p>

  <h2>How to Use This Guide</h2>
  <p>Start with the <a href="/guilin-yangshuo-itinerary-3-day" class="text-accent underline">3-day itinerary</a> — it covers the essential Guilin-to-Yangshuo experience. Then branch into specific interests: <a href="/li-river-cruise" class="text-accent underline">Li River cruise details</a>, <a href="/yangshuo-cycling-routes" class="text-accent underline">cycling routes</a>, <a href="/guilin-food" class="text-accent underline">food recommendations</a>, or <a href="/china-visa" class="text-accent underline">visa logistics</a>. Use the interactive <a href="/china-trip-builder" class="text-accent underline">trip builder</a> to plan a multi-city China route, and the <a href="/guilin-trip-cost-calculator" class="text-accent underline">cost calculator</a> to estimate your budget.</p>
  <p>The guide is designed to be read in any order — each page stands alone, but links to related content when you want to go deeper.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Found something outdated? Have a tip we should include? Notice a restaurant that closed or a ticket price that changed? We'd love to hear from you. Your ground-level reports help keep this guide useful for everyone.</p>
  <p>Email: <a href="mailto:hello@guilin-yangshuo.com" class="text-accent underline">hello@guilin-yangshuo.com</a></p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-stone-400">We respond to every email, usually within 48 hours. Corrections submitted with photos or receipts get priority.</p>
